// A snippet can already contain its whole symbol without the enclosure path ever touching
// it, and until now such a result carried no complete-symbol and so made no promise. That
// is the MIRROR of the clipped-unit false positive: one promised completeness for a
// fragment, this withheld it from a body that genuinely is whole, and both end with the
// agent opening a file it did not need to open.
//
// It also explains why the promise looked rare in the field (~12.8% of results in a real
// corpus): complete bodies are not rare, the SIGNAL was, because it is only assigned when
// widenSearchResultToEnclosure runs. Anything already complete at rank time was skipped.
//
// The test is BOUNDS, never bytes or display shape: the snippet's own line range must
// cover the symbol's. Three guards make that sound rather than merely plausible --
//   * budget truncation rewrites SnippetStartLine/EndLine as it shrinks
//     (compactSearchResultToBytes), so the bounds stay honest afterwards and cannot
//     describe lines that were dropped;
//   * the rendered line count must equal the span, so a body missing lines fails;
//   * the symbol must span at least two lines. The one place text is truncated WITHIN a
//     line is the single-line fallback, which collapses the span to one line; excluding
//     one-line symbols excludes that case entirely rather than trying to detect it.
// Results already carrying an elision or window signal are left alone: those are explicit
// statements that the body is partial, and this pass must never overrule them.
for index := range results {
	if markCompleteWhenSnippetCoversSymbol(&results[index]) {
		completeSymbols++
	}
}
stats.CompleteSymbols = completeSymbols
stats.LocatorSnippets = locators

