I've started one more background job, which only writes a document: a preregistration draft for **study 7**. It's a free offline test that runs only the search tool, with no model calls. It would answer two questions:

1. Do either of the crowding fixes find the right code more often without losing too many cases?
2. Does weakening the bonus for query words in a symbol's name help plain-English queries without hurting queries that use exact function names?

The draft follows study 6's rules:
- fresh repos that weren't used before or for tuning;
- queries written and frozen before any run;
- a rule limiting how many cases a fix may lose;
- checks that the expected cause actually changed, so a lucky score gain can't pass;
- a size calculation for how many queries are needed, and an estimate of the CPU cost.

It also checks in the code whether the fixes only affect search time. If they do, one index can serve every version and save most of the CPU. Running it would still need your and Codex's go-ahead.

Everything else is unchanged: PRs #317 and #96 are waiting on CI and your approval. Nothing paid has run, and the paid pilot still needs your spending cap.

