<!-- ================= 3 ================= -->
<section class="part" id="why">
  <h2>3. Why savings are hard</h2>
  <p class="kicker">Thousands of paired sessions taught us how token savings actually happen, and why "better search" alone doesn't produce them.</p>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>The graph is a 1:1 substitute</h4><p>Per session the agent made 2.71 graph calls and 2.65 fewer other calls: net −0.06 calls out of ~50.7. The biggest untouched pool is <code>cat</code>/<code>sed</code> (12.5 calls/session), which the graph displaces <i>not at all</i>. A 10% saving would need each graph call to replace about 3.3 calls.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Session length is the mechanism</h4><p>Across 93 paired instances, message-count change and token change correlate at <b>0.947</b>. Winners shortened sessions from 46 to 36 messages; losers lengthened them. Ten payload-quality metrics (line coverage, rank-1 correctness, editability…) failed to predict savings.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>It only pays in long sessions</h4><p>The graph saves by deleting pre-edit locate turns. Haiku spends ~9.4 turns locating and the graph removes 86% of them, worth ~12–16 points. Opus finishes in ~12 turns, so the payload costs more than it saves (+14% tokens). The fix/verify half of a session is untouched.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>More graph calls means a harder task</h4><p>Sessions needing 1–3 calls saved 22–25%; 5–6 calls saved nothing or lost (corr −0.17). Call count signals difficulty, so pushing usage up (e.g. via MCP) doesn't help.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>A perfect gate is worth only +8.3%</h4><p>Even an oracle that used the graph only on hard tasks would add 8.3 points. No pre-session feature predicts which tasks benefit: savings is not a property of the task (test-retest r = 0.014).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Cost is cache reads and output</h4><p>Cache reads are 96% of tokens but 49% of cost; output is 1.3% of tokens but 33% of cost. A 25% cost cut would need cache reads to fall ~48%, or output ~65%.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><b>The design implication.</b> Raw search quality is necessary but not enough. To move the north star, each graph answer has to <b>end the search</b>: deliver enough of the right code (whole bodies, the declaration line, the test command) that the agent's next calls disappear. That is why §7 targets the substitution ratio and session length, not just ranking.</div>

  <h3>Measurement traps we fell into (so the next result can't)</h3>
  <ul class="tight">
    <li><b>Completion-order bias.</b> Easy tasks finish first. The first 13 of 29 sessions read −35%; the remaining 16 read −2%. Never score a partial cell.</li>
    <li><b>Turn caps.</b> Tasks where only the baseline hit the cap looked like −42%. Only uncapped pairs are honest.</li>
    <li><b>Small samples.</b> A paired delta of +2.00 at n=400 became −0.39 at n=1,540. Deltas shrink with n, so report only at full sample, with a CI and median.</li>
    <li><b>Wrong token field.</b> The harness once summed the wrong usage block, a 36× worst case. <code>modelUsage</code> is canonical.</li>
    <li><b>Load pressure.</b> Running too many sessions at once inflates whichever arm runs under starvation, enough to flip the sign.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

