      <tr><td>Annotation line printed instead</td><td class="num">1</td><td><code>@Test</code> shown instead of the declaration.</td><td>Fixed in #316</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <h3>The two ranking mechanisms</h3>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>F2 · name-coverage dominance</h4><p>A symbol whose <i>name</i> contains query words gets up to +10, more than full body coverage (+7). So a symbol named from common query words can outrank the one whose body implements the behaviour. Among misses, 38% of targets share no core query term with their own name, vs 13% of hits. In a scoring emulator, dropping the weight rescued 6 misses and lost no hits. It was added on purpose for issue-style queries, so it needs a fresh test set.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>F1 · derived-row crowding</h4><p>Rows pulled in through callers, usages and neighbours score just below their parent (cap: parent − 0.01) and compete for the same 10 slots and per-file limits as direct hits. Real in source, but hand traces predict the drafted fixes would <i>not</i> rescue the two example cases. These rows also exist on purpose: one bridge measured +37.6% on a repo. Unproven, so not shipped.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--muted)">9 of the 66 ranking misses share almost no words with their target and need meaning-based search, not tuning. Study 7 is the drafted free offline test of F1 and F2 (version 3, awaiting review).</p>
</section>

<!-- ================= 6 ================= -->
<section class="part" id="memory">
  <h2>6. Graph + memory: how the north star gets past ~15%</h2>
  <p class="kicker">The graph is the structural substrate. The brain (memory) supplies text→code relevance and durable facts. The evidence says the big wins need both.</p>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h4>What the graph alone can do</h4><p>With the current design, every token lever measured and stacked gives a fair ceiling of about <b>+15%</b>. 25–35% was unreachable with the graph alone: the remaining cost lives in the fix/verify loop, which a locate tool can't shorten.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Where memory changes the math</h4><p>A precise up-front brief (the right symbols plus source, ranked) cut cost <b>42%</b> on druid and 30% on immutable. It also regressed when the brief was imprecise or the fix complex. Precision is the gate, and text→code precision is what vector/semantic memory supplies.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Durable facts</h4><p>The brain stores facts learned in earlier sessions (on the session's branch). An agent that already knows "the router lives here, tests run with this command" skips the whole locate phase. That is a session-length cut, the mechanism that actually moves tokens.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4>Honest status</h4><p>The brain's server side lives in an unmerged PR stack. Memory benchmarks are mixed: LoCoMo is a tie with mem0, LongMemEval is a win, BEAM is a loss. Graph+brain end-to-end has not been measured on coding tasks yet. That is the next frontier after the graph-only A/B.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= 7 ================= -->
<section class="part" id="road">
  <h2>7. The road to the north star</h2>
  <p class="kicker">Ordered by what unblocks the most. Each step has a measurable exit, and none is called done until the number says so.</p>
  <ol class="road">
    <li><div><h4>Measure it for real, on the subscription</h4><p>A paired A/B using Claude Code (<code>claude -p</code>) on your Max plan: graph vs no graph, identical prompts, explicit model <code>claude-sonnet-5-5</code>, launch build <code>0d92152d</code>. First an 8-pair mechanics trial (identity, accounting, cleanup), then 30+ pairs on fresh tasks with the method written down first. Primary: total tokens; cost, wall-clock and solve rate reported beside it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: a confidence interval on token savings. Status: setup drafted and reviewed; needs your confirmation that paid extra usage is off, plus a notional-cost stop.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Make each answer end the search</h4><p>Raise the substitution ratio above 1. That means certified whole bodies (<code>[complete]</code>), the declaration line on every shown row, and the test command (VERIFY) in the protected prefix. Measure follow-up reads per locate with <code>stats</code>' observed follow-up classes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: fewer follow-up reads per graph call in the A/B transcripts, plus shorter sessions.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Fix ranking where it measurably loses</h4><p>Test F2 (name weight) and F1 (crowding) in study 7: a free offline run on 12 fresh repos with a loss gate and a mechanism check. Ship an option only if it wins, doesn't lose too many cases, and the targeted cause actually falls.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: study 7 pass on its pre-registered rules. Status: plan v3 awaiting review.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Close the display gaps</h4><p>Recover declarations dropped from merged spans and gap fill, and find the default-path cause of cases 26 and 184. #317 already fixes the opt-in paths.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: tests that fail first and pass after; no byte growth.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Close the wall-clock gap</h4><p>The graph arm is ~8% slower. Cap or rethink the warm preindex path (keeps every grep match; 4–5× slower in one measurement), and keep default queries on the fast cold path.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: wall-clock parity in the A/B.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Add memory</h4><p>Graph + brain briefs for text→code relevance and durable facts, measured with the same paired harness. This is the only measured path past ~15%.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: graph+brain arm beats the graph-only arm on the north star ratio.</p></div></li>
    <li><div><h4>Keep the product honest</h4><p>Fix doc drift: <code>stats</code> docs, skill and status line still say "1:1 tokens saved"; a VERIFY cap comment says 320 B but the code uses 768; <code>--full-unit-top</code> is undocumented. Users should see measured numbers only.</p><p style="color:var(--muted)">Exit: no claim in product or docs that the benchmark doesn't support.</p></div></li>
  </ol>
</section>

