content.text:
Codex found five real defects in the Stage M memory build, all accepted and being fixed:
- graph data read live instead of frozen;
- a stop signal arriving during memory-building could still launch a cell;
- the standalone verifier could be fooled by tampering;
- the path filter let some absolute paths through;
- multi-line shell commands were mis-parsed.

I also made two design calls:
- **Edit names:** every function an earlier session touched is now named, still capped at 3 per file.
- **Command history:** commands that were themselves edits are dropped, since they just repeat the code changes.

The same agent is now applying the fixes, each with a test that fails when its safeguard is broken. It will then wire Stage M into the seal and launch scripts, stopping before anything costs money. Once its checks pass I'll send Codex the final version, and launch Stage M (about $3.7) if nothing new turns up. Nothing has been pushed or merged.

