Mostly yes on correctness, with three caveats. Nothing is merged yet, two PRs have real test failures, and none of these PRs makes the graph save tokens.

**What the open entire-graph PRs fix** (47 open in total):
- **Hangs:**
  - #298 (git grep hang);
  - #305 (infinite loop on echo router groups);
  - #307 (bounds every non-terminating fixed point);
  - #142 (indexing wall-clock ceiling).
- **Wrong answers:**
  - #270 (phantom members in Swift, Kotlin and Rust);
  - #280 (ambiguous calls labelled exact);
  - #281 (stray worktrees indexed);
  - #308 (Go routes);
  - #264 (every relation labelled extracted, inferred or ambiguous).
- **Honest numbers:**
  - #278, #279 and #304 (the "saved" stat that was about 1,800× inflated);
  - #301 (output that changed with machine speed).
- **Search quality:**
  - #291 (+8 top-3 hits on held-out tasks);
  - #283, #295 and #303 (agents see the right code body under tight budgets).
- **Integration:** #315 bundles the main launch fixes into one candidate.

**State:**
- **Merged:** none. Every one is blocked on **Entire Gates**, which is the approvals gate, so a human reviewer has to approve.
- **Real CI failures:** two PRs fail their status-line tests, **#277** on Linux and macOS and **#302** on macOS. These need fixing before anyone reviews them.
- **#315** is mergeable but targets a Codex integration branch, not main. Its last automated review timed out, so it's unreviewed rather than passed.

**What "solid" covers:** these PRs make the graph correct, bounded and honest. They don't make it a token saver: the pilot showed that cost is driven by agent turns, and these fixes don't remove turns. The proven value is better search than grep (79 vs 52 targets found).

Do you want me to fix the status-line failures in #277 and #302 so the whole set is green and ready for review?

