The Brain session checked the design's three findings against the live log and the source, and all three hold:
- its background watcher has never once rebuilt the code index;
- Brain and agents build two separate graph caches for the same commit;
- serving an old commit's index has to be opt-in, or Brain would stop rebuilding.

It corrected two counts (it counted log lines, the design counted failed refreshes; both are right) and one claim the design wrongly credited to its PR #482.

**What I'm building for it:** an opt-in flag that lets `graph context` answer from a recent commit's index, up to 5 commits back. The Brain session set five requirements, all now part of the design and the build brief:
- the code can never accidentally save a stale index as current;
- staleness comes back as a structured field, not text;
- **the list of changed files is always complete, or there's no answer at all**, so Brain never says "no callers" when the callers sit in exactly the files it skipped;
- both sides use the same three things to decide when an index is current;
- the separate cache-alignment question stays its own change.

